<Base title="The buffer pool" description="What a database’s page cache should hold, how it shares memory with the operating system’s cache, how large to make it, which bulk reads push the working set out, and why a restart or failover starts slow, for PostgreSQL, InnoDB, SQL Server and SQLite.">
  <article class="visual-essay relational-design storage-essay">
    <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>The buffer pool</h1><TitleLine type="Companion" version="PostgreSQL 18, with InnoDB (MySQL 8.4), SQL Server and SQLite comparisons" /></header>
<ArticleBody>

    <p>Every page a query reads or changes passes through the <strong>buffer pool</strong>, the cache of pages a database keeps in memory. <a href={`${storage}#buffers`}>Storage and durability</a> introduced it: a page already in memory is a <strong>hit</strong>, a page fetched from storage is a <strong>read</strong>, and once every slot is taken, each read has to <strong>evict</strong> a page to make room.</p>
    <p>A hit costs a memory lookup and a read can cost a trip to the drive, and the pool is usually far smaller than the database. When it holds the pages the application keeps coming back to, most requests never leave memory. When it loses them, to a nightly report, a restart or a failover, the same queries wait on storage and the application slows down although nothing is broken. This article is about keeping the right pages there: what the pool should hold, how much memory to give it, and how to get it warm again.</p>
    <p>The examples follow the shop from Storage and durability: every product page view looks up the <code>products</code> table through its primary-key index, and a nightly report reads the whole <code>page_views</code> table. The measured numbers come from PostgreSQL 18 on a laptop with a deliberately tiny pool, and show which pages stay in memory, not how fast anything runs.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="holds"><h2 id="holds">What the pool holds</h2>
      <p>The pool holds table pages and index pages side by side, and they compete for the same slots. Finding P7 through the primary-key index takes four page requests in a typical three-level B-tree: the index’s root, a branch, the leaf holding P7’s entry, and the table page holding the row. In InnoDB the table <em>is</em> the primary-key index, so the same lookup takes three. InnoDB also keeps its <a href={`${mysql}innodb-undo-logs.html`}>undo log</a> pages in the pool.</p>
      <p>The upper levels of an index are tiny. In the test database, the primary-key index on the 1.2-million-row <code>page_views</code> table has 3,279 leaf pages and only 13 above them. Every lookup passes through those few, so they are almost always cached, and which leaves and table pages are in memory decides most hits and misses.</p>
      <p>The pages a workload keeps using over some period, an hour, say, are its <strong>working set</strong>. The shop’s is the whole <code>products</code> table and its index, 501 pages or about 4 MB. A real application’s might be the last month of orders, the active customers and the upper levels of every index, but not five years of history nobody reads. The size of the database says little about how big the pool needs to be; the working set says a great deal.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="two-caches"><h2 id="two-caches">Two caches: the pool and the operating system’s</h2>
      <p>PostgreSQL reads its files through the operating system, which keeps its own cache of recently used file data, the <strong>page cache</strong>. A miss in shared buffers, PostgreSQL’s name for its pool, can therefore be answered from memory. In the test, reading the shop’s 444 table pages back from the page cache took 2 to 3 microseconds a page, far quicker than any drive.</p>
      <p>Two things follow. A page can be held twice, in shared buffers and in the page cache, and memory given to one is memory the other can’t use. That is why PostgreSQL’s <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-SHARED-BUFFERS`}>documentation</a> suggests starting at 25% of RAM on a dedicated server and says more than 40% is unlikely to work better. And PostgreSQL’s counters can’t tell you whether a read reached the drive: <code>blks_read</code> and <code>EXPLAIN</code>’s <code>read=</code> count misses in shared buffers, whoever answered them.</p>
      <p>InnoDB takes the other route. In MySQL 8.4, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-parameters.html#sysvar_innodb_flush_method`}><code>innodb_flush_method</code></a> defaults to <code>O_DIRECT</code> on Linux, so reads bypass the page cache. Each page is in memory once, a miss is a read from the device, and the pool can take most of the machine: the <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool.html`}>manual</a> says up to 80% of memory is often assigned on a dedicated server. SQL Server also manages most of the machine’s memory itself, growing its pool as it needs pages.</p>
      <p>SQLite has no server, so each connection keeps its own small <a href={`${sqlite}pragma.html#pragma_cache_size`}>page cache</a>, about 2 MB by default, and relies on the operating system’s cache, which all connections share. A connection throws its cached pages away when another connection has changed the file, so where others write often, a larger cache helps less than its size suggests.</p>
      <p>This changes what a restart costs. Restarting the PostgreSQL server, or an application holding SQLite connections, empties their own caches but leaves the page cache warm, so the first misses come from memory. Restarting MySQL or SQL Server leaves nothing to fall back on. Rebooting, moving to a new virtual machine or failing over starts every cache empty.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="scans"><h2 id="scans">Scans that push the working set out</h2>
      <p>Once the pool is full, every read evicts something. The hard case is a job that reads many pages exactly once. The shop’s nightly report reads all 14,815 pages of <code>page_views</code>, more than seven times the test pool. Under a plain least-recently-used policy, each page the report reads becomes the newest, and once it has read as many pages as the pool holds, the shop’s pages are gone.</p>
      <p>The servers guard against this in different ways. PostgreSQL gives a sequential scan of a large table a small <strong>ring</strong> of buffers to reuse, so in the test the report left 96 of its pages in the pool and the shop’s pages untouched. InnoDB puts newly read pages into an <a href={`${mysql}innodb-performance-midpoint_insertion.html`}>old sublist</a> and promotes them only if they are used again more than a second later, so a scan’s pages fall off the end without reaching the working set. SQLite’s small caches use plain LRU, and rely on the operating system’s cache behind them.</p>
      <p>The guards cover specific operations, not every heavy read. In PostgreSQL the ring applies to sequential scans, <code>VACUUM</code> and bulk loads. The same report run through an index scan uses the normal policy, and in the test it read 18,093 pages and left nothing of the shop behind. So a plan change from a sequential scan to an index scan can change what a report does to everyone else. A bulk <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> also falls back to the normal policy, because the pages it reads become dirty. The clock sweep and the ring are followed in detail in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/architecture/#eviction">Inside PostgreSQL</a>, with a figure that runs the report against four policies.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="sizing"><h2 id="sizing">Choosing a size</h2>
      <h3 id="knee">Where more memory stops helping</h3>
      <p>More memory helps until the pool holds the working set, then helps much less. Take a modelled workload: a 100 GB database receiving 40,000 page requests a second, where 99% fall on an 8 GB working set and the other 1% spread evenly over the remaining 92 GB. Assume the pool keeps the working set first. Each row is arithmetic from those assumptions, not a measurement.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Modelled hit rate and reads per second by pool size">
        <table>
          <caption>Hit rate and reads per second for the modelled workload</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Pool size</th><th scope="col">Hit rate</th><th scope="col">Reads per second</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>2 GB</td><td>24.8%</td><td>30,100</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4 GB</td><td>49.5%</td><td>20,200</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6 GB</td><td>74.3%</td><td>10,300</td></tr>
            <tr><td>8 GB</td><td>99.0%</td><td>400</td></tr>
            <tr><td>16 GB</td><td>99.1%</td><td>365</td></tr>
            <tr><td>32 GB</td><td>99.3%</td><td>296</td></tr>
            <tr><td>64 GB</td><td>99.6%</td><td>157</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Below 8 GB, every working-set page the pool can’t hold will be wanted again soon, so reads climb steeply: half the working set costs fifty times the reads. Above 8 GB, extra memory holds rarely used pages, and each gigabyte saves fewer reads than the last. That bend, the <strong>knee</strong>, is the size worth finding. Real workloads have no sharp edge between hot and cold pages, so the knee is a curve, but the shape holds.</p>
      <p>The direct way to find it is to try several sizes and watch reads per second. InnoDB can <a href={`${mysql}innodb-buffer-pool-resize.html`}>resize its pool while running</a>, so raise it in steps and stop when <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_reads</code> per second stops falling. PostgreSQL needs a restart for each size, so run the experiment on a replica or a copy with the same traffic. Two cheaper readings help in PostgreSQL. Each buffer has a <strong>usage count</strong>, raised each time the page is used and lowered as the eviction sweep passes, which <code>pg_buffercache</code> shows. If a steady share of buffers sits at 0 or 1 through a busy period, the pages in repeated use fit. The counts are explained in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/architecture/#eviction">Inside PostgreSQL</a>. And with <code>track_io_timing</code> on, reads that take microseconds are coming from the page cache, so the working set fits in the two caches together.</p>

      <h3 id="memory">What else needs the memory</h3>
      <p>The pool shares the machine with everything else, and running out is worse than a smaller pool. If the total exceeds physical memory, the operating system swaps, and a “hit” can become a disk read the database can’t see; or it kills a process to free memory, which may be the database.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong> gives each sort and hash operation up to <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-WORK-MEM`}><code>work_mem</code></a>, 4 MB by default and often raised for reporting, and a query can run several such operations in each of many sessions. With <code>work_mem</code> at 32 MB, 100 active queries each running two sorts could use 6.4 GB. Maintenance work, connections and the page cache need the rest.</li>
        <li><strong>InnoDB</strong> allocates about 10% more than <code>innodb_buffer_pool_size</code> for the pool’s own structures. On a 64 GB server, an 80% pool costs about 56 GB, leaving 8 GB for the operating system, connections and their buffers.</li>
        <li><strong>SQL Server</strong> takes memory up to <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/database-engine/configure-windows/server-memory-server-configuration-options">max server memory</a>, whose default is effectively unlimited. Set it, so the operating system and anything else on the machine keep enough.</li>
        <li><strong>SQLite’s</strong> cache is per connection, so the total is the cache size times the open connections. Twenty connections at 64 MB each can use up to 1.28 GB.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="starting-points">Starting points</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Starting sizes for the page cache by engine"><table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Engine</th><th scope="col">Start at</th><th scope="col">Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>25% of RAM on a dedicated server, rarely past 40%</td><td>Changing it needs a restart. A larger pool holds more dirty pages, so raise <code>max_wal_size</code> with it to spread checkpoint writes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>InnoDB</td><td>Up to 80% on a server that runs nothing else</td><td><a href={`${mysql}innodb-dedicated-server.html`}><code>--innodb-dedicated-server</code></a> sets it from detected memory, to 75% above 4 GB.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQL Server</td><td>Set max server memory to leave the operating system several gigabytes</td><td>The default is effectively unlimited.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SQLite</td><td>The default 2 MB per connection</td><td>Raise <code>cache_size</code> when a connection repeatedly reads more than that and the misses cost something; set it each time a connection opens.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <h3 id="too-small">Signs the pool is too small</h3>
      <p>The clearest sign is a steady stream of reads under a steady workload. In InnoDB, a sustained rate of <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_reads</code> and a growing <code>Innodb_buffer_pool_wait_free</code>, which counts waits for a clean slot, both point to a pool smaller than the working set. In PostgreSQL, check how long the reads take before acting. If they come from the page cache in microseconds, a larger pool moves pages from one cache to the other and gains little. A full pool on its own means nothing; every pool fills up.</p>
      <p>Whether a stream of reads matters depends on what the storage can serve. On a cloud volume, reads above the provisioned IOPS are throttled. Read latency is the better signal: while storage keeps up, the time per read stays flat as reads rise, and when it climbs, the storage is near its limit. The hit rate is a poor guide to any of this, for reasons set out in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/#hit-rate">Understanding database performance</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="cold"><h2 id="cold">A cold pool after a restart or failover</h2>
      <p>After a restart the pool is empty, and every working-set page has to be read once before it can be hit. Until then the database is slow in a way that looks like a fault but is only a cache filling.</p>
      <p>Take a 64 GB server with 16 GB of shared buffers holding a 16 GB working set: about 2.1 million pages of 8 kB. If the storage serves 5,000 random reads a second, reading each once takes about seven minutes. In practice the most used pages come back within seconds and the long tail takes much longer. If the page cache survived the restart, those reads are memory copies and the slow period is short. After a reboot, they go to the drive.</p>

      <h3 id="saving">Saving the pool’s contents and loading them back</h3>
      <p>InnoDB <a href={`${mysql}innodb-preload-buffer-pool.html`}>does this by default</a>. At shutdown it writes the page numbers of the most recently used 25% of the pool (<code>innodb_buffer_pool_dump_pct</code>) to a small file, and at startup it reads those pages back in the background. The list is written at shutdown, so after a crash it is whatever was saved last; setting <code>innodb_buffer_pool_dump_now</code> on a schedule keeps it recent.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL does it with the <a href={`${docs}pgprewarm.html`}><code>pg_prewarm</code></a> extension. Its background worker saves the list of cached pages every five minutes and at shutdown, and reads them back after a restart, so the list is recent even after a crash. In the test, a restart with it on left the first product query all hits; without it, the same query read all 444 table pages again.</p>
      <Sql code={prewarm} label="Prewarming PostgreSQL's shared buffers" />
      <p>SQLite has nothing to save: its caches are small and refill quickly, and the cache that matters is the operating system’s.</p>

      <h3 id="failover">After a failover</h3>
      <p>A standby’s pool was filled by different work: the pages that replaying the primary’s changes touched, plus whatever queries ran there. If no reads went to it, it becomes the primary partly cold, at the moment the system is already under stress.</p>
      <p>Three things help. Sending real read traffic to standbys keeps their pools close to the primary’s. Running <code>pg_prewarm</code> on the most important tables can be a step in the failover runbook; automatic prewarming does nothing at that moment, because promotion isn’t a restart. And timing the slow period in a failover drill tells you how long it lasts before you need to know. The rest of that decision is covered in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-failover/">Replication and failover</a>.</p>
    </section>

